{"no":6,"file_no":6,"title":"Vectors and their Products","area":"Mathematics","hours":"3","lang":"en","words":1896,"read_min":11,"figures":3,"examples":0,"has_questions":true,"toc":[{"id":"s1","title":"6.1 Vectors in plane and space, algebra, linear combination, dependence","kind":""},{"id":"s2","title":"6.2 Product of two vectors, scalar and vector product, scalar triple product","kind":""},{"id":"s3","title":"Formula and fact sheet","kind":""},{"id":"s4","title":"Chapter summary","kind":""},{"id":"s5","title":"Practice questions","kind":"qs"}],"html":"<p>Vectors carry about <strong>3 questions</strong> (our estimate).</p><p class=\"ck\">The booklet's model question — the projection of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b=2\\hat i+3\\hat j-2\\hat k</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a=\\hat i+2\\hat j+3\\hat k</span> — is typical: a dot product, a cross product, or a scalar triple product, computed from components.</p><p class=\"ck\">The whole chapter reduces to four formulas used fluently.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\">\n<div class=\"bt\">What the paper asks from this chapter</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Components, magnitude, unit vector, section formula, collinearity.</li>\n<li>Dot product: <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=ab\\cos\\theta</span>; projection of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a|}</span>.</li>\n<li>Cross product: <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|=ab\\sin\\theta</span>, area of a triangle <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{2}|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span>.</li>\n<li>Scalar triple product: volume, coplanarity (<span class=\"m\">[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]=0</span>).</li>\n<li>Linear dependence: two vectors are dependent iff parallel; three iff coplanar.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"s1\">6.1 Vectors in plane and space, algebra, linear combination, dependence</h2>\n<p>A vector has magnitude and direction. For <span class=\"m\">\\vec a=a_1\\hat i+a_2\\hat j+a_3\\hat k</span>: <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|=\\sqrt{a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2}</span> and the <strong>unit vector</strong> is <span class=\"m\">\\hat a=\\vec a/|\\vec a|</span>.</p><p class=\"ck\">Direction cosines <span class=\"m\">\\frac{a_1}{|\\vec a|},\\frac{a_2}{|\\vec a|},\\frac{a_3}{|\\vec a|}</span>. The vector <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\vec b-\\vec a</span> (head minus tail).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Triangle law: <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\overrightarrow{AC}</span>; the parallelogram law; <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\pm\\vec b|^2=a^2+b^2\\pm2\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Section formula:</strong> point dividing <span class=\"m\">AB</span> in the ratio <span class=\"m\">m:n</span> internally is <span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{m\\vec b+n\\vec a}{m+n}</span> (externally <span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{m\\vec b-n\\vec a}{m-n}</span>); midpoint <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\vec a+\\vec b}{2}</span>; centroid <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\vec a+\\vec b+\\vec c}{3}</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Collinear points</strong> <span class=\"m\">A,B,C</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AC}=k\\,\\overrightarrow{AB}</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Linear combination:</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\vec r=x\\vec a+y\\vec b+z\\vec c</span>. A set is <strong>linearly dependent</strong> if some non-trivial combination is zero: two vectors iff parallel; three iff coplanar (<span class=\"m\">[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]=0</span>); any four in space always. Three non-coplanar vectors form a basis of space.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"s2\">6.2 Product of two vectors, scalar and vector product, scalar triple product</h2>\n<h3>Scalar (dot) product</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=ab\\cos\\theta=a_1b_1+a_2b_2+a_3b_3</span>; commutative and distributive. <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\cdot\\hat i=1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\cdot\\hat j=0</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec a=|\\vec a|^2</span>.</p><p class=\"ck\"><strong>Perpendicular iff <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=0</span>.</strong>\n<span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a||\\vec b|}</span>. <strong>Projection</strong> of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a|}</span> (a scalar); the vector component is that times <span class=\"m\">\\hat a</span>.</p><p class=\"ck\"><strong>Work</strong> <span class=\"m\">=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec d</span>. <span class=\"m\">(\\vec a+\\vec b)\\cdot(\\vec a-\\vec b)=a^2-b^2</span>.</p>\n<h3>Vector (cross) product</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times\\vec b=ab\\sin\\theta\\,\\hat n</span> (right-hand rule), anticommutative; <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times\\hat j=\\hat k</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat j\\times\\hat k=\\hat i</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat k\\times\\hat i=\\hat j</span>.</p>\n<p><div class=\"dm\"><span class=\"m d\">\\vec a\\times\\vec b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\hat i&amp;\\hat j&amp;\\hat k \\\\ a_1&amp;a_2&amp;a_3 \\\\ b_1&amp;b_2&amp;b_3\\end{vmatrix}.</span></div></p>\n<p><span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|^2=a^2b^2-(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b)^2</span> (Lagrange). <strong>Parallel iff <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times\\vec b=\\vec 0</span>.</strong> Area of a parallelogram with sides <span class=\"m\">\\vec a,\\vec b</span> is <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span>; of the triangle, half.</p><p class=\"ck\">A unit vector perpendicular to both: <span class=\"m\">\\pm\\dfrac{\\vec a\\times\\vec b}{|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|}</span>. Moment of a force: <span class=\"m\">\\vec r\\times\\vec F</span>.</p>\n<h3>Triple products</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]=\\vec a\\cdot(\\vec b\\times\\vec c)=</span> the 3×3 determinant of components; cyclic invariant, changes sign when two vectors swap.</p><p class=\"ck\"><strong>Volume</strong> of the parallelepiped <span class=\"m\">=|[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]|</span>; tetrahedron <span class=\"m\">=\\frac{1}{6}|[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]|</span>.</p><p class=\"ck\"><strong>Coplanar iff <span class=\"m\">[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]=0</span>.</strong> Vector triple product: <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times(\\vec b\\times\\vec c)=\\vec b(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec c)-\\vec c(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b)</span> (\"BAC minus CAB\").</p>\n<div class=\"box caution\">\n<div class=\"bt\">Common mistake</div>\n<p>The dot product is a <strong>scalar</strong>, the cross product a <strong>vector</strong>. <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times(\\vec b\\times\\vec c)\\ne(\\vec a\\times\\vec b)\\times\\vec c</span>. The projection can be negative.</p>\n</div>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/b05825d9338b1a0f44e2.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> Tug of war: each team pulls with a force that has a size and a direction</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" alt=\"diagram\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Resultant of 120 N, 100 N and 80 N at 120 degrees spacing</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 6.1.</b> Adding vectors. (a) In a tug of war the rope tension is a vector: the pull has a size and a direction, and the result depends on both. (b) Three ropes tied to a ring pull with 120 N at 0 degrees, 100 N at 120 degrees and 80 N at 240 degrees. Adding components gives Rx = 30 N and Ry = 17.32 N, so the resultant has magnitude 34.64 N at 30 degrees. Drawn head to tail, the three forces and the resultant form a closed polygon. A fourth force of 34.64 N at 210 degrees would give equilibrium. Any three vectors in a plane are linearly dependent.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) Joy Agyepong, CC BY-SA 4.0.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/86175d3571e8c15917e1.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> A click torque wrench: turning effect equals r cross F</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" alt=\"diagram\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Torque of a 150 N push on a 0.40 m handle vs angle</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 6.2.</b> The vector product. (a) A torque wrench measures the turning effect of a force, which is the vector product tau = r x F, with magnitude r F sin(theta). (b) For a handle length r = 0.40 m and a force F = 150 N at 60 degrees to the handle, the cross product of the vectors r = (0.40, 0, 0) and F = 150 (cos 60, sin 60, 0) is (0, 0, 51.96), so the torque is 51.96 N m along the axis of the bolt. The check r . tau = 0 and F . tau = 0 confirms that the result is perpendicular to both vectors. The torque is greatest at 90 degrees (60 N m) and zero at 0 and 180 degrees.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) No machine-readable author provided. Heron, CC BY-SA 3.0.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/df925d247ab0c092e302.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> A road-gradient warning sign: a car on a slope</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" alt=\"diagram\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Weight and displacement on a 20 per cent grade</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 6.3.</b> The dot product as work. (a) A steep-gradient sign warns drivers that gravity has a large component along the road. (b) Take a 1,200 kg car on a 20 per cent grade (rise 1 for every 5 along the ground), so the angle is 11.31 degrees. The weight is W = (0, -11,760) N. Along 100 m of road the displacement is d = (98.06, 19.61) m, and W . d = -230,633 J, which equals -m g h for a rise of 19.61 m. The part of the weight pulling along the road is m g sin(theta) = 2306 N, and this is the force the brakes must resist.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) Bidgee, CC BY-SA 2.5 au.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<div class=\"hk\"><h2 id=\"s3\">Formula and fact sheet</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>angle</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta=\\frac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{ab}</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>projection of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a|}</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>triangle area</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{2}|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lagrange</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|^2=a^2b^2-(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b)^2</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>volume</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">|[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]|</span>; tetrahedron <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{6}</span> of it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>triple product</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times(\\vec b\\times\\vec c)=\\vec b(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec c)-\\vec c(\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b)</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"chsum\"><h2 id=\"s4\">Chapter summary</h2>\n<ul class=\"cols2\">\n<li>Head minus tail gives <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AB}</span>; the section formula weights the opposite end.</li>\n<li>Dot product zero means perpendicular; cross product zero means parallel; triple product zero means coplanar.</li>\n<li>Projection is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b}{|\\vec a|}</span>, a signed scalar.</li>\n<li>Areas come from <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span>, volumes from the scalar triple product.</li>\n</ul>\n</section><h2 id=\"s5\" class=\"qs\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Set A — Recall (10 questions)</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq\">\n<li data-key=\"c\">For <span class=\"m\">\\vec a=2\\hat i+3\\hat j+\\hat k</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\vec b=\\hat i-\\hat j+2\\hat k</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b</span> equals<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">7</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-1</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The magnitude of <span class=\"m\">3\\hat i+4\\hat j-12\\hat k</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">13</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">19</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">11</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\"><span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times\\hat j</span> equals<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">0</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-\\hat k</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\hat k</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The vectors <span class=\"m\">2\\hat i+\\lambda\\hat j+\\hat k</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat i-2\\hat j+3\\hat k</span> are perpendicular for <span class=\"m\">\\lambda=</span><span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">5/2</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">3/2</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">The area of the parallelogram with adjacent sides <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+\\hat j</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat j+\\hat k</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">If <span class=\"m\">[\\vec a\\,\\vec b\\,\\vec c]=0</span>, the three vectors are<span class=\"opts c2\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">coplanar</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">parallel</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">mutually perpendicular</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">collinear</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">The angle between <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+\\hat j</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat j</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">30^\\circ</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">60^\\circ</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">90^\\circ</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">45^\\circ</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The midpoint of <span class=\"m\">A(2,4,6)</span> and <span class=\"m\">B(4,0,2)</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">(6,4,8)</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">(3,2,4)</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">(1,2,2)</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">(3,4,2)</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">The unit vector along <span class=\"m\">3\\hat i-4\\hat j</span> is<span class=\"opts c2\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{3\\hat i-4\\hat j}{25}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">3\\hat i-4\\hat j</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{3\\hat i+4\\hat j}{5}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\dfrac{3\\hat i-4\\hat j}{5}</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">If <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|=5</span> and <span class=\"m\">|\\vec b|=3</span>, then <span class=\"m\">(\\vec a+\\vec b)\\cdot(\\vec a-\\vec b)</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">16</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">34</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">4</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">25</span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"ans box note\"><summary>Answers and explanations — Set A</summary><div class=\"ans-body\">\n<p class=\"ansline\"><strong>1.</strong> (c) — <span class=\"m\">2-3+2=1</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>2.</strong> (a) — <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{9+16+144}=13</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>3.</strong> (d) — right-hand rule: <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times\\hat j=\\hat k</span> (<span class=\"m\">\\hat j\\times\\hat i=-\\hat k</span>).</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>4.</strong> (b) — <span class=\"m\">2-2\\lambda+3=0\\Rightarrow\\lambda=\\frac{5}{2}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>5.</strong> (c) — <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\times\\vec b=(1,-1,1)</span>, magnitude <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>6.</strong> (a) — a zero scalar triple product means the vectors lie in one plane.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>7.</strong> (d) — <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>8.</strong> (b) — average of the coordinates.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>9.</strong> (d) — divide by the magnitude 5.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>10.</strong> (a) — <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|^2-|\\vec b|^2=25-9=16</span>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Set B — Understanding (7 questions)</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq\">\n<li data-key=\"b\">The projection of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b=2\\hat i+3\\hat j-2\\hat k</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a=\\hat i+2\\hat j+3\\hat k</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">2/\\sqrt{17}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">2/\\sqrt{14}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">2/7</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-2/7</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">If <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|=3</span>, <span class=\"m\">|\\vec b|=4</span> and the angle between them is <span class=\"m\">30^\\circ</span>, then <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">12</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">6\\sqrt{3}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">The vectors <span class=\"m\">2\\hat i-\\hat j+\\hat k</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+2\\hat j-3\\hat k</span> and <span class=\"m\">3\\hat i+\\lambda\\hat j+5\\hat k</span> are coplanar for <span class=\"m\">\\lambda=</span><span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">4</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-1</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-4</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The area of the triangle with vertices <span class=\"m\">(1,0,0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0,1,0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0,0,1)</span> is<span class=\"opts c2\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{2}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{3}{2}</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">The volume of the parallelepiped with edges <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+\\hat j</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat j+\\hat k</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat k+\\hat i</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">4</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">A unit vector perpendicular to both <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+\\hat j</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat j+\\hat k</span> is<span class=\"opts c2\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\hat i+\\hat j+\\hat k}{\\sqrt{3}}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\hat i-\\hat j+\\hat k}{\\sqrt{3}}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\hat i-\\hat j-\\hat k}{3}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\hat i+\\hat j-\\hat k}{\\sqrt{3}}</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The value of <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times(\\hat j\\times\\hat i)</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\hat j</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">0</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">-\\hat j</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\hat k</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"ans box note\"><summary>Answers and explanations — Set B</summary><div class=\"ans-body\">\n<p class=\"ansline\"><strong>1.</strong> (b) — <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=2+6-6=2</span>, <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|=\\sqrt{14}</span>. The projection of <span class=\"m\">\\vec b</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\vec a</span> divides by <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>2.</strong> (c) — <span class=\"m\">3\\cdot4\\sin30^\\circ=6</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>3.</strong> (d) — the determinant is <span class=\"m\">28+7\\lambda=0</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>4.</strong> (a) — <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}=(1,1,1)</span>; half of <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>5.</strong> (c) — <span class=\"m\">\\begin{vmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0 \\\\ 0&amp;1&amp;1 \\\\ 1&amp;0&amp;1\\end{vmatrix}=1+1=2</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>6.</strong> (b) — <span class=\"m\">(\\hat i+\\hat j)\\times(\\hat j+\\hat k)=\\hat i-\\hat j+\\hat k</span>, magnitude <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>7.</strong> (a) — <span class=\"m\">\\hat j\\times\\hat i=-\\hat k</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times(-\\hat k)=\\hat j</span>. Check with BAC–CAB: <span class=\"m\">\\hat j(\\hat i\\cdot\\hat i)-\\hat i(\\hat i\\cdot\\hat j)=\\hat j</span>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Set C — Application (6 questions)</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq\">\n<li data-key=\"b\">The points <span class=\"m\">(1,1,1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(2,3,4)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3,5,\\lambda)</span> are collinear when <span class=\"m\">\\lambda=</span><span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">7</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">8</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The work done by <span class=\"m\">\\vec F=2\\hat i+3\\hat j+\\hat k</span> in moving a particle from <span class=\"m\">(1,1,1)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(3,2,4)</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">10</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">12</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">8</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">9</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">If <span class=\"m\">\\vec a,\\vec b</span> are unit vectors at <span class=\"m\">60^\\circ</span> to each other, <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a+\\vec b|</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2}</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3}</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">If <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a|=2</span>, <span class=\"m\">|\\vec b|=3</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\vec a\\cdot\\vec b=3</span>, then <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">9</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">3\\sqrt{3}</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The vectors <span class=\"m\">2\\hat i+3\\hat j</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat i+\\lambda\\hat j</span> are linearly dependent for <span class=\"m\">\\lambda=</span><span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">2/3</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">3/2</span></span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\"><span class=\"m\">1/6</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The volume of the tetrahedron with vertices <span class=\"m\">O</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1,0,0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0,2,0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0,0,3)</span> is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">1</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"ans box note\"><summary>Answers and explanations — Set C</summary><div class=\"ans-body\">\n<p class=\"ansline\"><strong>1.</strong> (b) — <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=(1,2,3)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\overrightarrow{AC}=(2,4,\\lambda-1)</span> must be <span class=\"m\">2\\overrightarrow{AB}</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda-1=6</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>2.</strong> (a) — displacement <span class=\"m\">(2,1,3)</span>: <span class=\"m\">4+3+3=10</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>3.</strong> (d) — <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a+\\vec b|^2=1+1+2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=3</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>4.</strong> (d) — <span class=\"m\">|\\vec a\\times\\vec b|^2=36-9=27</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>5.</strong> (b) — parallel: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2}{1}=\\frac{3}{\\lambda}</span>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>6.</strong> (a) — <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{6}|1\\cdot2\\cdot3|=1</span>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Group D — Model answers for long questions (10 marks each, for deeper revision)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}=(1,2,3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{b}=(2,-1,1)</span> find the scalar product, angle, vector product, parallelogram area and projection. <span class=\"marks\">[10]</span></li>\n<li>Use the scalar triple product to test coplanarity and to find volumes; find the work done by a force and the torque of a force. <span class=\"marks\">[10]</span></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Model answer D1: Scalar and vector products (10 marks)</h3>\n<p><strong>Introduction.</strong> The <strong>scalar (dot) product</strong> is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}=\\lvert\\mathbf{a}\\rvert\\lvert\\mathbf{b}\\rvert\\cos\\theta=a_1b_1+a_2b_2+a_3b_3</span>; the <strong>vector (cross) product</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}</span> has magnitude <span class=\"m\">\\lvert\\mathbf{a}\\rvert\\lvert\\mathbf{b}\\rvert\\sin\\theta</span> and is perpendicular to both vectors.</p>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Solution.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Working</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">2-2+3</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">3</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\lvert\\mathbf{a}\\rvert,\\ \\lvert\\mathbf{b}\\rvert</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{14},\\ \\sqrt6</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">3.742,\\ 2.449</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Angle <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{84}}=0.327</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">70.9^\\circ</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot1-3(-1),\\ 3\\cdot2-1\\cdot1,\\ 1(-1)-2\\cdot2)</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">(5,5,-5)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Area of parallelogram</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\lvert\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\rvert=\\sqrt{75}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">8.66</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Projection of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{b}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\lvert\\mathbf{b}\\rvert}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt6}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">1.22</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Checks.</strong> <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=5+10-15=0</span> and <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\mathbf{b}=10-5-5=0</span>, so the result is perpendicular to both. The area of the triangle with these sides is half: <span class=\"m\">4.33</span>.</p>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Properties.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}</span>, but <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}</span>.</li>\n<li>Two non-zero vectors are perpendicular if <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}=0</span> and parallel if <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\cdot\\hat i=1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat i\\times\\hat j=\\hat k</span>.</li>\n<li>Section formula: the point dividing <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{b}</span> in the ratio <span class=\"m\">m:n</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{m\\mathbf{b}+n\\mathbf{a}}{m+n}</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The dot product measures how much two vectors point together and the cross product measures the area they span.</p>\n<h3>Model answer D2: Triple product, work and torque (10 marks)</h3>\n<p><strong>Introduction.</strong> The <strong>scalar triple product</strong> <span class=\"m\">[\\mathbf{a}\\ \\mathbf{b}\\ \\mathbf{c}]=\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})</span> equals the determinant of the three vectors. Its absolute value is the volume of the parallelepiped.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Coplanarity.</strong> Three vectors are coplanar if <span class=\"m\">[\\mathbf{a}\\ \\mathbf{b}\\ \\mathbf{c}]=0</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}=(1,2,3)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{b}=(2,-1,1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{c}=(1,1,2)</span>:</p>\n<p><div class=\"dm\"><span class=\"m d\">\\begin{vmatrix}1&amp;2&amp;3\\\\2&amp;-1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1&amp;2\\end{vmatrix}=1(-2-1)-2(4-1)+3(2+1)=-3-6+9=0.</span></div></p>\n<p>So they are <strong>coplanar</strong>: indeed <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{c}=\\frac35\\mathbf{a}+\\frac15\\mathbf{b}</span>, since <span class=\"m\">\\frac35+\\frac25=1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac65-\\frac15=1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac95+\\frac15=2</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Volume.</strong> For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{c}=(3,1,0)</span>: the determinant <span class=\"m\">=1(0-1)-2(0-3)+3(2+3)=-1+6+15=20</span>. The parallelepiped has volume <span class=\"m\">20</span> and the tetrahedron has volume <span class=\"m\">\\frac{20}{6}=3.33</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Work done.</strong> <span class=\"m\">W=\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{F}=(2,3,1)\\ \\text{N}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{d}=(3,-1,2)\\ \\text{m}</span>: <span class=\"m\">W=6-3+2=5\\ \\text{J}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Torque.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\tau=\\mathbf{r}\\times\\mathbf{F}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{r}=(2,1,0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{F}=(1,-1,3)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\tau=(1\\cdot3-0,\\ 0-2\\cdot3,\\ 2(-1)-1\\cdot1)=(3,-6,-3)</span>, with magnitude <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{9+36+9}=\\sqrt{54}=7.35\\ \\text{N m}</span>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coplanarity test</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">[\\mathbf{a}\\ \\mathbf{b}\\ \\mathbf{c}]=0</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">0</span> (coplanar)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parallelepiped volume</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\lvert[\\mathbf{a}\\ \\mathbf{b}\\ \\mathbf{c}]\\rvert</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">20</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Work</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">5\\ \\text{J}</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torque</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{r}\\times\\mathbf{F}</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">7.35\\ \\text{N m}</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Identity.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf{a}\\times(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=\\mathbf{b}(\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{c})-\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b})</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The triple product gives volume and a coplanarity test, and the two products model work and torque in physics.</p>"}